### Describe the feature you'd like to request

[io-ts](https://github.com/gcanti/io-ts) is a decoding/encoding library based on [fp-ts](https://github.com/gcanti/fp-ts) functional programming patterns.

Currently, the behaviour when using it with tRPC is a bit weird, it just doesn't work as it should, I believe it can be improved since I've seen a few issues already about this topic.

Its syntax is very similar to `Zod` but validators returns an `Either<Error, Data>`.

### Describe the solution you'd like to see

Currently. When using io-ts as a validator, it works like this.

```ts
import * as t from "io-ts";
import * as E from "fp-ts/Either";
import { pipe } from "fp-ts/function";

const exampleCodec = t.type({
  text: t.union([t.string, t.null]),
});

export const exampleRouter = router({
  hello: publicProcedure.input(exampleCodec.decode).query(({ input }) =>
    pipe(
      input,
      E.fold(
        (error) => {
          console.log(error);
        },
        (response) => {
          return { greeting: `Hello ${response.text ?? "world"}` };
        }
      )
    )
  ),
});

```

Here, we can see we pass the `decode` function to the input of the procedure and makes the `input` type `E.Left<t.Errors> | E.Right<{text: string | null}>`.
No problems here. This is 100% expected. Some minor improvements could be that if its an `E.Left` case, it fallback to a TRPC error.

The big issue comes when I want to use `useQuery()` on this procedure. Because the input it asks for is an `Either`. I would like to be able to pass the structure of the `E.Right` case. I can access that structure dynamically using `t.TypeOf<typeof exampleCodec>`

### Describe alternate solutions

If it seems too much effort, not do it.

